{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792490005,"nonce":"X4qZGpi+e9XIGJOMhpPVRgtol70=","message":"Landed in c0923ea00fdb8aff0683230484b647940c3598ca.\n\nImplemented recursive return inference without lowering function bodies twice. The existing interned/Fx dependency worklist now carries a return-dependency flag on each edge; scratch sets are reused, so a second dependency map/body is unnecessary. Semantic symbol IDs distinguish self calls from shadowed names. Whole direct self-call returns are excluded from inference, discarded self calls permit void inference, concrete base returns settle the result, and a complete function with no other returned candidate infers never. Deferred self-call nodes receive the final result type before shared lowering. Cyclic dependencies in arithmetic, conditional and mutual return expressions report Type errors on function names with an annotation hint. Function-value cycles and the void operator are kept distinct from strict return-type cycles.\n\nThe shared compiler represents never as a zero-result native signature. Returned never expressions end in unreachable after evaluating the call, on both CFG and nested-return lowering paths. There is no fabricated integer or void value handed to a value-returning caller. Tests inspect this HIR and compile never functions on actual native tiers without executing infinite recursion.\n\nThe work also fixed the recursive fresh-result leak described in the earlier comment. Automatic release proves ownership by pruning storage-returning candidates to a stable set; manual release retains its previous policy. Tests assert one caller release across a recursive factory and borrowed alias, no release of a returned object in its callee, and correct borrowed alias propagation through a mutual cycle. The collector remains disabled.\n\nValidation:\n- Full TypeScript tests passed with and without LLVM enabled: 25 tests in each configuration, including pinned acceptance/output checks.\n- Five new recursion tests cover settled typed AST nodes, native never HIR, formatted strict-cycle diagnostics, explicit remaining gaps and actual interpreter-first/Cranelift/LLVM execution. The native test verifies all relevant functions install at LLVM before checking finite calls.\n- Shared compiler drop tests: 22 passed. Expression lowering (17), block-value control flow (3), implicit aggregate returns (4) and owned-phi releases (4) passed, including their interpreter/Cranelift/LLVM runtime legs.\n- LLVM-enabled Clippy with all targets, no dependencies and -D warnings passed. Formatting and staged whitespace checks passed. Python harness tests: 7 passed.\n- Added 16 pinned TypeScript 6.0.2/Node oracle fixtures, all passing. Full live comparison: 130 fixtures across interpreter, baseline and LLVM-selected profiles, with 375 PASS and 15 XFAIL and no unexpected results. The runner verified the executable remained unchanged during comparison. Binary SHA256: d33ccee8630ddec2aaa939e4a1e5ddf76ca1b858ba757a8ae263ab8e9196a4cf. Actual native promotion is separately tested; selecting a harness profile alone is not proof of entry into that tier.\n- pending/inferred_cycle and pending/inferred_recursive_strict pass in all three profiles. KNOWN_FAILURES shrank from 7 entries to 5, with no added expected failures.\n\nRemaining pinned gaps are unions, arrays, structural width views, heterogeneous inferred return unions and possibly undefined inferred results. Broader recursive function-value types, inference through local aliases, unannotated recursive generics and diverging expressions used as scalar values remain explicit implementation work under parent 6875fc2949f83d682861ecab814e0ff82c824f5990620b0d64ca0340ebb026e5. No execution-speed benchmark was taken.","files":null}]}